{"parentUuid":"200fed80-e9d2-4373-847c-11c092830ece","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a6179c12-9735-4004-930a-6d2755933449","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011eDkVVGV5MQrFbv2ntksje","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nname: cp-alias-blocks-overwrites\ndescription: \"cp on this Mac is GNU coreutils aliased to -i, and -f does not cancel it; use command cp or an Edit to overwrite a file\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: reference\n  originSessionId: d86754f7-7a8e-4c81-8133-a50a412c8c6c\n  modified: 2026-09-16T12:36:47.654Z\n---\n\n\u0060cp\u0060 in this shell is \u0060nocorrect cp -i\u0060, and it resolves to GNU coreutils cp\n(\u0060/opt/homebrew/opt/coreutils/libexec/gnubin/cp\u0060, ahead of \u0060/bin/cp\u0060 on PATH), not BSD cp.\n\nOverwriting an existing file therefore prompts and silently does nothing in a\nnon-interactive Bash call — it prints \u0060overwrite <path>? (y/n [n]) not overwritten\u0060 and\nexits 0, so the copy looks like it succeeded. Adding \u0060-f\u0060 does **not** help: BSD cp\ndocuments \u0060-f\u0060 as overriding \u0060-i\u0060, GNU cp does not.\n\n\u0060rm\u0060 is aliased to \u0060-i\u0060 the same way: \u0060rm <file>\u0060 prints \u0060remove <path>?\u0060 and deletes\nnothing, exiting 0. Use \u0060/bin/rm -f\u0060 (checked 2026-09-10, deleting probe scratch files).\n… 9 more lines","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"79710b3a-b0d3-4892-a5e9-5966595236a0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T21:53:50.439Z"}
