{"parentUuid":"99471889-2c15-4b67-9eac-3166ec293a00","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"033f48ed-5008-485d-b0ea-ea090d630d45","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LPbsaBn82dGuqe9Rjwk1HG","type":"tool_result","content":"App/LowTalker/BenchWindow.swift:237:            }, events: { [weak self] in self?.shown(\u00240) }, ended: { [weak self] in self?.ended(\u00240) })\nApp/LowTalker/BenchWindow.swift:294:    private func ended(_ ending: BenchEnding) {\nREADME.md:185:The app runs the loop: hold the chord, speak, release, and what was said is inserted at the cursor. Key-down marks where the utterance begins on the audio ring and reads which app is in front, and the engine hears the press as it is captured. Words land as they are confirmed: each time a pass confirms words past those already inserted, the new ones are inserted while the key is still down, and key-up inserts the rest of the transcript, so no word is inserted twice. An insert the input method refuses during a press stops that press's inserts, and a press that lapses or loses audio inserts nothing more; either way the words already inserted stay, and the report says how many there were. Sessions are heard and inserted on one serial queue, so two presses in quick succession insert in the order they were spoken however long the engine takes on either. Each session is one line in the unified log under the \u0060dictation\u0060 category: how many words were heard, how long after key-up, how many inserts landed while the key was down and how long after key-down the first did, and which app's cursor took them.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e5e67ad2-74c8-4d6a-aabb-b3654d0f7869","timestamp":"2026-10-03T21:08:09.227Z"}
