{"parentUuid":"9b058d76-77da-45e2-96af-77ddc8bb83c0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"2661b7f4-d8f3-4d9a-aaea-59693a1bbb6c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TDbqdAofosw4uew6o8QnZ1","type":"tool_result","content":"27:The network build of the app serves transcription with the model it already holds for dictation, so a caller costs no second copy in memory. It answers OpenAI's \u0060POST /v1/audio/transcriptions\u0060 and its Realtime transcription socket, \u0060ws://.../v1/realtime?intent=transcription\u0060, at port 8610, on loopback unless the config's \u0060[serve]\u0060 table names an interface and a token (see the config below). So a Pipecat \u0060OpenAISTTService\u0060 pointed at the base URL the menu's Server line shows, or an \u0060OpenAIRealtimeSTTService\u0060 pointed at the same URL as \u0060ws://\u0060 plus \u0060/realtime\u0060, transcribes through it. The socket streams a turn's words as deltas while its audio is still arriving and answers each commit with the transcript; it takes 24 kHz PCM16, as the spec requires, and only turns the client commits, so \u0060turn_detection\u0060 must be null. It transcribes up to four uploads at once and holds up to four sockets open, answering any more of either with a 429, which Pipecat retries for an upload and not for a socket, so a pipeline whose socket is refused stops transcribing. A socket keeps reading while its turns wait for the engine, as they do behind a dictation hold, so a busy engine delays its transcripts, which come in the order the turns were committed. It refuses an upload holding over fifteen minutes of audio, or an append that would take what a socket holds past fifteen minutes: the audio of its turns not yet transcribed, and a second for each turn. So an engine that stays behind a socket's audi\n… 2 more lines","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e3d68bea-0928-429b-8434-00803a2222a7","timestamp":"2026-10-04T07:05:52.806Z"}
